\f1\i0 \'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Did he?\'92 Tony asked and Sarah heard the deception in his voice. \'91Are you sure?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes. I heard him distinctly. Look; it\'92s obviously important. You truly know nothing about it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony\'92s blood chilled. 
\f2\i Coincidence
\f1\i0 . Sarah registered his hesitation. \'91It\'92s nothing!\'92 Tony defended while glancing furtively at Sarah\'92s knees.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s nothing?\'92 Sarah was becoming frantic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Just some old boot leather I picked up from a dead guy on a street. 
\f2\i That
\f1\i0  nothing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked, trying to process. \'91You stole a dead guy\'92s boot?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No!\'92 Tony protested, but felt ashamed anyway. \'91Look, it\'92s just a piece of junk, ok? It\'92s still down there, I think, in the footwell.\'92 Her nodded at her knees.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Oh, that\'92s what he couldn\'92t take his eyes off. 
\f1\i0 Sarah hesitated, grimaced and held her breath while she felt around the floor, wondering what kind of gunk she\'92d get on her hands. She almost missed it; it felt like part of the floor mat until she realised it was loose. Tentatively, she picked at it, got a finger nail under, and retrieved it. By nighttime streetlight, she examined it, both sides.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This can\'92t be what the fuss is about \'85 Whoah!\'92 The leathery disc in her hand shimmered a rainbow. \'91Did you see that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Tony kept his eyes on the road and his hands doing manic things with the steering wheel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It \'85 flashed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony risked a glance, saw only the daggy circle of hide he\'92d tossed aside days ago. \'91Yeah. I thought it did that before. It\'92s just a trick of the light.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flipped it over and over and tilted it at various angles, trying to catch the light again. She twisted it, just a little, testing its flexibility. The she tossed it in the air, like a coin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The van crashed and pivoted over its front wheel, landed on its passenger side, slid across the street and collided with a kebab cart. Tabouleh and sauces, red and white, smeared over the van\'92s windscreen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, in the seat closest to the collision, didn\'92t get thrown much as the van toppled over. She would have suffered no injury at all if the Tony\'92s flailing body hadn\'92t come down upon her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You hit a pothole.\'92 Sarah, containing her fury, enunciated the accusation slowly and precisely.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nope. I travel these roads every day. There\'92s no pothole back there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then what did you hit?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I dunno. Wombat?\'92 Tony tried to grin but his jaw hurt. \'91C\'92mon. We gotta move.\'92 He lifted himself off Sarah, climbed in back, stumbled over dislodged equipment and supplies, and threw the payload doors open.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah followed. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Around front, Tony appraised the windscreen mess. \'91Tax! I hate garlic sauce!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked for damage, evidence of the original collision. There appeared no damage to the front wheel, the wheel arch, or the axel, as if some titan had put the van on its side as easily as a chess piece.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91C\'92mon.\'92 Tony strode away, already some metres down the road.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Where are you going?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91To listen to some karaoke.\'92
